{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"destructors-raii","title":"Destructors & RAII","group":"Classes & Objects","order":28,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A destructor <code>~T()</code> runs automatically when an object's lifetime ends. <strong>RAII</strong> ties a resource (memory, file, lock, socket) to an object so the destructor always releases it — even when an exception is thrown.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>RAII</strong> — <em>Resource Acquisition Is Initialization</em>: acquire the resource in the constructor, release it in the destructor. The object's scope becomes the resource's lifetime.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trash\"></i>Destructor basics</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nstruct Noisy {\n    std::string name;\n    explicit Noisy(std::string n) : name(std::move(n)) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"ctor \" &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\n    ~Noisy() { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"dtor \" &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Noisy a(\"a\");\n    {\n        Noisy b(\"b\");\n        Noisy c(\"c\");\n    }                             // c then b destroyed here — reverse order\n    Noisy d(\"d\");\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \"end of main\\n\";\n}                                 // d then a</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>ctor a\nctor b\nctor c\ndtor c\ndtor b\nctor d\nend of main\ndtor d\ndtor a</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file\"></i>Writing an RAII wrapper</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;cstdio&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;\n\nclass File {\n    std::FILE* f_;\npublic:\n    File(const char* path, const char* mode) : f_(std::fopen(path, mode)) {\n        if (!f_) throw std::runtime_error(\"cannot open file\");\n    }\n    ~File() { std::fclose(f_); std::cout &lt;&lt; \"file closed\\n\"; }\n    File(const File&amp;) = delete;              // one owner only\n    File&amp; operator=(const File&amp;) = delete;\n    std::FILE* get() const { return f_; }\n};\n\nvoid process() {\n    File out(\"raii_demo.txt\", \"w\");\n    std::fputs(\"hello\\n\", out.get());\n    throw std::runtime_error(\"something failed\");   // destructor still closes the file\n}\n\nint main() {\n    try { process(); } catch (const std::exception&amp; e) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"caught: \" &lt;&lt; e.what() &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\n    std::remove(\"raii_demo.txt\");\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>file closed\ncaught: something failed</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>Memory</h3><p><code>std::unique_ptr</code>, <code>std::shared_ptr</code>, <code>std::vector</code>, <code>std::string</code></p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i>Locks</h3><p><code>std::lock_guard</code>, <code>std::unique_lock</code>, <code>std::scoped_lock</code></p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file-text\"></i>Files &amp; streams</h3><p><code>std::ifstream</code> / <code>std::ofstream</code> close on destruction</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-split\"></i>Threads</h3><p><code>std::jthread</code> joins on destruction</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield\"></i>Scope guards</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n\ntemplate &lt;class F&gt;\nclass ScopeExit {                       // run any cleanup when the scope ends\n    F fn_;\npublic:\n    explicit ScopeExit(F fn) : fn_(std::move(fn)) {}\n    ~ScopeExit() { fn_(); }\n    ScopeExit(const ScopeExit&amp;) = delete;\n    ScopeExit&amp; operator=(const ScopeExit&amp;) = delete;\n};\n\nint main() {\n    int depth = 0;\n    {\n        ++depth;\n        ScopeExit restore([&amp;] { --depth; std::cout &lt;&lt; \"restored\\n\"; });\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; \"depth \" &lt;&lt; depth &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    }\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \"depth \" &lt;&lt; depth &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>depth 1\nrestored\ndepth 0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Destructors should never throw. They're implicitly <code>noexcept</code>; if one throws while the stack is already unwinding due to another exception, <code>std::terminate</code> is called.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why doesn't C++ need a garbage collector?</summary><div><p>Because resources are owned by objects with deterministic lifetimes: when the owner goes out of scope its destructor frees them. Containers and smart pointers apply RAII to memory; the same technique also handles non-memory resources (files, locks, sockets) that a GC wouldn't close promptly.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When is a destructor called?</summary><div><p>Automatic objects: at the end of their scope (including during exception unwinding). Static objects: after <code>main</code> returns. Heap objects: on <code>delete</code> (or when the owning smart pointer releases them). Temporaries: at the end of the full expression. Members and bases: after the containing object's destructor body, in reverse declaration order.</p></div></details></section>"}